Here's what I found by reading `docs/models.md`, `docs/custom-provider.md`, `docs/providers.md`, and the `custom-provider-anthropic` example.

## Short answer

Pi has **no built-in "rotate between two OpenAI accounts" feature**. The built-in `openai` provider stores exactly one key in `auth.json`, and there is no automatic key rotation or fallback. But you have three good options, ordered by simplicity.

## Option 1 — Two custom providers in `models.json` (recommended, simplest)

Register two providers that both hit `https://api.openai.com/v1` but read different env vars. `/model` then shows them as separate entries you can switch between with one keystroke.

`~/.pi/agent/models.json`:

```json
{
  "providers": {
    "openai-work": {
      "baseUrl": "https://api.openai.com/v1",
      "api": "openai-completions",
      "apiKey": "$OPENAI_API_KEY_WORK"
    },
    "openai-personal": {
      "baseUrl": "https://api.openai.com/v1",
      "api": "openai-completions",
      "apiKey": "$OPENAI_API_KEY_PERSONAL"
    }
  }
}
```

Then export the two keys and pick either in `/model`. If you skip `models`, built-in OpenAI models are preserved under each provider; if you add a `models` array it **replaces** the built-in list for that provider (per `docs/models.md`).

This is exactly the pattern used in the Vercel AI Gateway / Google AI Studio examples in `docs/models.md`.

## Option 2 — An extension that rotates keys on error

If you want automatic rotation (e.g., fall over to account B when A returns 429), write a small extension. The two relevant docs are:

- `docs/custom-provider.md` — how to `pi.registerProvider(...)` and how to write `streamSimple`
- `examples/extensions/custom-provider-anthropic/` — full working template (OAuth + API key + custom streaming)

Skeleton (TypeScript extension in `~/.pi/agent/extensions/openai-rotate.ts`):

```ts
import type { ExtensionAPI } from "@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent";
import { readFile } from "node:fs/promises";

// Minimal approach: register a custom OpenAI-compatible provider whose
// apiKey is a shell command that picks the next key from a file/secret store.
// Pi re-runs shell commands on every request (see docs/models.md "Value Resolution").
export default function (pi: ExtensionAPI) {
  pi.registerProvider("openai-rotate", {
    baseUrl: "https://api.openai.com/v1",
    api: "openai-completions",
    apiKey: "!bash -c 'cat ~/.pi/agent/openai-keys.txt | shuf -n1'",
  });
}
```

For real rotation-on-429 you need `streamSimple` so you can catch the error and retry with key B — see `docs/custom-provider.md` "Custom Streaming API" and the reference links to `openai-completions.ts` in pi-mono.

## Option 3 — Switch keys manually

Three quick ways, no config files:

- **Per-invocation flag**: `pi --api-key sk-...` (overrides env and auth.json, per `docs/usage.md`)
- **Env var**: `OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-... pi`
- **Edit `~/.pi/agent/auth.json`** — the `openai` entry takes a single `key` field. Or store two entries and edit the `openai` one when you want to switch.

`auth.json` also supports `!command` so you can do `{ "openai": { "type": "api_key", "key": "!op read 'op://Personal/OpenAI/api'" } }` and swap the 1Password reference.

## A few things that won't work, worth knowing

- **Two entries under the same `openai` key in `auth.json`**: not supported. It's a single object with one `key` field (`docs/providers.md` "Auth File").
- **Built-in rotation/fallback across accounts**: doesn't exist. `modelFallbackMessage` (`docs/sdk.md`) is about handoff between *providers* when a model isn't available, not about API key rotation.
- **`/login` for OpenAI**: just stores the one key — same as writing `auth.json` by hand.

## My recommendation

For two accounts you actually use yourself, **Option 1** (two custom providers in `models.json`) is the cleanest. You get two entries in `/model` like `openai-work/gpt-5` and `openai-personal/gpt-5`, and switching is a keypress away. The file reloads every time you open `/model`, so you can edit env vars without restarting pi (`docs/models.md`: "The file reloads each time you open `/model`. Edit during session; no restart needed.").

If you want true auto-rotation, go with **Option 2** — but plan to write a non-trivial `streamSimple` to handle 429s and retry.

