Good — that changes things. Here's what I found after digging into `dist/core/auth-storage.js` and the `@earendil-works/pi-ai/oauth` exports.

## Why the API-key answer doesn't directly apply

The `openai-codex` provider is hardcoded as a single OAuth-registered provider (id `"openai-codex"`, baseUrl `https://chatgpt.com/backend-api`, `dist/providers/openai-codex.js`). Its token is stored in `auth.json` under that one id, and the storage layer enforces one entry per provider id:

```js
// dist/core/auth-storage.js
this.data[providerId] = credential;  // overwrites
```

So you can't have two `openai-codex` entries in the same `auth.json`. You need two distinct provider ids.

## Three ways to do it, in order of how cleanly they fit the docs

### Option 1 — Two separate `pi` instances via `PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR` (simplest, zero code)

`getAgentDir()` in `dist/config.js:412` honors `$PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR` (it's `APP_NAME.toUpperCase() + "_CODING_AGENT_DIR"`, and `APP_NAME = "pi"`). Each agent dir has its own `auth.json`, `models.json`, sessions, etc.

```bash
# terminal 1
PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR=~/.pi-agent-work pi
# inside: /login → openai-codex (work account)

# terminal 2
PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR=~/.pi-agent-personal pi
# inside: /login → openai-codex (personal account)
```

No extension, no `/model` switching. Trade-off: you can't use both accounts in one session.

### Option 2 — Custom-provider extension that re-uses the Codex OAuth flow (one session, both accounts) — recommended

The OAuth implementation is exported from `@earendil-works/pi-ai/oauth` (`dist/utils/oauth/index.js`):

```text
loginOpenAICodex, refreshOpenAICodexToken, openaiCodexOAuthProvider
```

An extension can register a second provider with a different id and the same OAuth behavior, giving you a second slot in `auth.json`. `registerProvider` accepts an `oauth: { name, login, refreshToken, getApiKey }` block exactly like this (from `docs/custom-provider.md` §OAuth Support).

`~/.pi/agent/extensions/two-codex.ts`:

```ts
import type { ExtensionAPI } from "@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent";
import {
  loginOpenAICodex,
  refreshOpenAICodexToken,
  OPENAI_CODEX_BROWSER_LOGIN_METHOD,
  OPENAI_CODEX_DEVICE_CODE_LOGIN_METHOD,
} from "@earendil-works/pi-ai/oauth";

// Same models the built-in openai-codex provider registers, with
// (provider-name, model-id) prefixes so /model shows them separately.
// Copy the full list from dist/providers/openai-codex.models.js for your version.
const CODEX_MODELS = [
  { id: "gpt-5.5",          name: "GPT-5.5",          reasoning: true,  input: ["text", "image"], contextWindow: 400000, maxTokens: 128000, cost: { input: 0, output: 0, cacheRead: 0, cacheWrite: 0 } },
  { id: "gpt-5.5-codex",    name: "GPT-5.5 Codex",    reasoning: true,  input: ["text", "image"], contextWindow: 400000, maxTokens: 128000, cost: { input: 0, output: 0, cacheRead: 0, cacheWrite: 0 } },
  { id: "gpt-5.1-codex-max",name: "GPT-5.1 Codex Max",reasoning: true,  input: ["text", "image"], contextWindow: 400000, maxTokens: 128000, cost: { input: 0, output: 0, cacheRead: 0, cacheWrite: 0 } },
];

function registerCodexClone(pi: ExtensionAPI, id: string, displayName: string) {
  pi.registerProvider(id, {
    name: displayName,
    baseUrl: "https://chatgpt.com/backend-api",
    api: "openai-codex-responses",
    models: CODEX_MODELS,
    oauth: {
      name: `OpenAI Codex (${displayName})`,
      async login(callbacks) {
        // Mirrors the built-in flow. /login will show browser vs device-code.
        const method = await callbacks.onSelect({
          message: `Login to ${displayName}:`,
          options: [
            { id: OPENAI_CODEX_BROWSER_LOGIN_METHOD,        label: "Browser (localhost callback)" },
            { id: OPENAI_CODEX_DEVICE_CODE_LOGIN_METHOD,   label: "Device code" },
          ],
        });

        if (method === OPENAI_CODEX_DEVICE_CODE_LOGIN_METHOD) {
          // Use the device-code flow; it pushes the user code via onDeviceCode
          // and waits internally for the user to complete auth.
          const { loginOpenAICodexDeviceCode } = await import("@earendil-works/pi-ai/oauth");
          // The helpers take their own callback shapes, so for brevity here we
          // wrap a no-op onDeviceCode. (You'd usually let the user pick browser
          // and skip this branch in a clone — see note below.)
          return loginOpenAICodexDeviceCode({
            onDeviceCode: () => {},
          });
        }

        return loginOpenAICodex({
          onAuth: ({ url, instructions }) =>
            callbacks.onAuth({ url, instructions }),
          onPrompt: (p) => callbacks.onPrompt({ message: p.message }),
        });
      },
      async refreshToken(creds) {
        return refreshOpenAICodexToken(creds.refresh);
      },
      getApiKey(creds) {
        return creds.access;
      },
    },
  });
}

export default function (pi: ExtensionAPI) {
  registerCodexClone(pi, "openai-codex-work",     "Work account");
  registerCodexClone(pi, "openai-codex-personal", "Personal account");
}
```

After loading the extension:

- `/login` lists both `OpenAI Codex (Work account)` and `OpenAI Codex (Personal account)` alongside the built-in `openai-codex` (if you haven't logged out of it). Log into each in turn.
- `/model` then shows entries like `Work account/gpt-5.5`, `Personal account/gpt-5.1-codex-max` — switch with a keypress.
- `auth.json` ends up with two independent entries:
  ```json
  {
    "openai-codex":          { "type": "oauth", "access": "...", "refresh": "...", "expires": 1730000000000 },
    "openai-codex-work":     { "type": "oauth", "access": "...", "refresh": "...", "expires": 1730000000000 },
    "openai-codex-personal": { "type": "oauth", "access": "...", "refresh": "...", "expires": 1730000000000 }
  }
  ```

Caveats worth knowing up front:

- **OAuth callback port** — the Codex flow listens on `http://127.0.0.1:1455/auth/callback` (overridable with `PI_OAUTH_CALLBACK_HOST`). If you try to log into both clones in the same `/login` session they will race on the port. Log in to one, finish, then log in to the other. After login, there's no port contention — token refresh is a server-side POST.
- **Model list drift** — I listed three models for illustration. Copy the exact list from your installed `dist/providers/openai-codex.models.js` so pricing and capability flags match.
- **No automatic rotation on rate-limit** — the extension registers two independent providers; you still pick the model manually. If you want auto-failover on 429 you'd add a `streamSimple` wrapper as described in `docs/custom-provider.md` §Custom Streaming API.

### Option 3 — Manual swap in one `auth.json` (no extension, but tedious)

1. `/login openai-codex` with account A.
2. Copy the `openai-codex` entry aside, e.g. `cp ~/.pi/agent/auth.json ~/.pi/agent/auth.json.work`.
3. `/logout openai-codex`, then `/login openai-codex` with account B.
4. `cp ~/.pi/agent/auth.json.personal ~/.pi/agent/auth.json` to switch back.

Useful if you only need to switch a few times a week and don't want to maintain an extension.

## Recommendation

If you regularly use both accounts and want them switchable mid-session, **Option 2**. If you only need them in separate windows/tasks and never interleaved, **Option 1** is the smallest possible change (one env var, no extension). The `auth.json` data model itself doesn't support two entries under the same provider id, so any "true rotation" inside a single session has to go through one of these two paths.

